Description
Goal
One orchestrating agent, built from separable subagents, covering the documentation lifecycle across GitLab-hosted docs (MkDocs and other repos): discover work that needs a doc update, identify the affected docs, draft the update, track it in Jira, and commit the draft to a branch for review. It consolidates tools that already work for two people.

Operating model
The agent runs in two stages with a human gate between them and another after.

Propose. On a schedule, the agent scans the configured source Jira projects, decides which tickets need documentation work, and creates a tracking (proposal) ticket in the team's tracking Jira project. The proposal links the source tickets and names the manual(s) it believes need updates, with the reason and a confidence level.
Human gate 1. A person reviews the proposal. If they agree, they move the ticket to the approval status (for example "Approved for Draft"). That status change is the trigger. If they disagree, they move it to a rejected status and record why.
Draft. On the trigger, the agent reads the source tickets (and, in Phase 7, the code committed for them), works out where in the manual(s) the change belongs, creates a branch in GitLab named for the ticket, and commits the proposed changes. It moves the ticket to a review status (for example "Draft Review"), describes what it changed, and links the branch.
Human gate 2. A person reviews the draft and approves or rejects it. Nothing is merged or published by the agent.
Train. Rejections and corrections at either gate are recorded with a reason, and a person approves changes to the team's guidance so the agent behaves differently next time (Phase 4).
The agent does not route SME feedback; that is a technical writer task.

Logging. All logging goes to the tracking tickets in Jira (the tickets created in Phase 2). Each comment records what the agent did or decided and why. There are no log files and no local state on disk; what has already been proposed is rebuilt from the tracking tickets and their links to the source tickets. If Jira cannot be reached, the run stops and reports a failure through the scheduler.

GitLab only. This build works against GitLab. Confluence is neither read nor written in Phases 0 to 7, and the one non-GitLab store, the Velocity Slate API site, is its own phase (Phase 9).

New manuals (Phase 8). A new manual is started by a person, not the agent. The technical writers, working with the team, decide a manual is needed and gather the inputs: all the relevant Jira tickets plus Confluence team documentation. There is no minimum or maximum; the agent does not detect or propose new manuals. The writers create the GitLab project from their manual template, add it to the configuration, and create a new-manual ticket listing the inputs. When they approve it, the agent reads the inputs (Confluence read-only) and drafts the first content, following the template, on a ticket-named branch. The agent never creates repositories or edits configuration. Until then, for tickets that fit no configured manual, the agent only reports them as unmatched.

The agent is configured per team and per manual. Each manual names its own Jira and GitLab instance (US or NULSC), project and layout, so one team can span instances (Velocity is US; GNF and MMA are NULSC; others will follow). Adding a team, a manual or a source project is a configuration change, because new documentation needs arise at any time.

Where things live. All manuals for this work are in GitLab. Confluence is not a manual store and is never written; it is read, read-only, only as source material for new manuals (Phase 8). The Slate site that stores API information is in GitHub Enterprise and is handled in Phase 9. Code for the source tickets can be in GitLab or GitHub depending on the repo, so the code host is configured separately from the documentation store.

